WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.754 --> 00:00:02.417
- Try to have your own sound.

00:00:02.417 --> 00:00:03.776
Try to invent, you know,

00:00:03.776 --> 00:00:04.753
dig into yourself.

00:00:04.753 --> 00:00:07.553
Music only became interesting

00:00:07.553 --> 00:00:09.348
when people stepped over boundaries.

00:00:09.348 --> 00:00:12.175
You don't want to be stuck
in just doing one thing.

00:00:12.175 --> 00:00:13.548
As an artist you've got to develop.

00:00:13.548 --> 00:00:16.113
As I notice, if I don't enjoy myself,

00:00:16.113 --> 00:00:17.881
the crowd's more flat as well.

00:00:17.881 --> 00:00:19.794
And I want to stress that the word passion

00:00:19.794 --> 00:00:21.539
is probably the the
most important in life.

00:00:21.539 --> 00:00:22.878
The past is who you are,

00:00:22.878 --> 00:00:25.776
but you cannot change the past.

00:00:25.776 --> 00:00:26.693
- Hello Believe Nation.

00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:27.814
My name is Evan Carmichael.

00:00:27.814 --> 00:00:29.066
My one word is believe,

00:00:29.066 --> 00:00:30.860
and I believe that
entrepreneurs will solve

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:33.337
all of the worlds major problems.

00:00:33.337 --> 00:00:34.553
So to help you on your journey,

00:00:34.553 --> 00:00:35.556
today we're going to learn

00:00:35.556 --> 00:00:37.146
from legendary Dutch DJ,

00:00:37.146 --> 00:00:38.565
Armin van Buuren,

00:00:38.565 --> 00:00:41.775
and my take on his top
10 rules of success.

00:00:41.775 --> 00:00:43.342
Rule number one is my personal favorite,

00:00:43.342 --> 00:00:45.852
and I'd love to know which
one you guys liked the best.

00:00:45.852 --> 00:00:46.685
And as always,

00:00:46.685 --> 00:00:47.518
as you're watching,

00:00:47.518 --> 00:00:49.256
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:49.256 --> 00:00:51.108
please leave it in the comments below.

00:00:51.108 --> 00:00:53.639
Put quotes around it so
other people can be inspired;

00:00:53.639 --> 00:00:55.514
you might win a prize as well.

00:00:55.514 --> 00:00:56.869
And also, when you write it down,

00:00:56.869 --> 00:00:59.380
it's much more likely to
stick for yourself as well.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:00.941
Enjoy.

00:01:00.941 --> 00:01:03.688
(jet engine)

00:01:03.688 --> 00:01:06.271
(upbeat music)

00:01:13.784 --> 00:01:16.873
- Look at other DJs, see
why their sets are good,

00:01:16.873 --> 00:01:18.812
but try to have your own sound.

00:01:18.812 --> 00:01:20.185
Try to invent, you know.

00:01:20.185 --> 00:01:21.416
Dig into yourself,

00:01:21.416 --> 00:01:23.076
and see who you are.

00:01:23.076 --> 00:01:25.846
And kind of bring
something new to the table.

00:01:25.846 --> 00:01:27.713
Because if you're just copying other DJs,

00:01:27.713 --> 00:01:29.133
then you'll just be a copy.

00:01:29.133 --> 00:01:30.700
So you've got to find your own identity

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.315
before you go out there.

00:01:32.315 --> 00:01:34.932
If you do what other
people are doing out there,

00:01:34.932 --> 00:01:37.599
then you're not going to stand out.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:41.902
I always say deadmau5 is an example.

00:01:41.902 --> 00:01:42.735
Hate him or love him.

00:01:42.735 --> 00:01:43.765
But what he's done,

00:01:43.765 --> 00:01:45.064
is you can put your finger on it.

00:01:45.064 --> 00:01:47.268
You know, putting on a weird mouse head,

00:01:47.268 --> 00:01:49.185
making excellent music,

00:01:50.535 --> 00:01:51.738
that makes a difference,

00:01:51.738 --> 00:01:53.289
that makes you stand out.

00:01:53.289 --> 00:01:54.322
And,

00:01:54.322 --> 00:01:55.606
I think the most important thing,

00:01:55.606 --> 00:01:57.114
is if you want to be successful,

00:01:57.114 --> 00:01:59.928
that you do whatever you love,

00:01:59.928 --> 00:02:00.926
and you look at other artists.

00:02:00.926 --> 00:02:02.054
Of course you look at other artists.

00:02:02.054 --> 00:02:03.006
I do that as well.

00:02:03.006 --> 00:02:04.413
You get inspired by other people's music.

00:02:04.413 --> 00:02:06.590
But try to make it your own.

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:10.301
I mean, think of all these
people sitting at home,

00:02:10.301 --> 00:02:12.643
and you're doing a concert in their city.

00:02:12.643 --> 00:02:15.056
Ask yourself, why would these people,

00:02:15.056 --> 00:02:16.047
when they're at home--

00:02:16.047 --> 00:02:17.056
You know they're
comfortable, they're warm,

00:02:17.056 --> 00:02:19.602
they're having a pizza,
they're watching TV.

00:02:19.602 --> 00:02:21.619
Why would they come out to see you?

00:02:21.619 --> 00:02:23.705
You know, answer that
question in two sentences,

00:02:23.705 --> 00:02:26.654
and that's your answer right there.

00:02:26.654 --> 00:02:27.932
I mean why would you go see deadmau5?

00:02:27.932 --> 00:02:29.305
Because he's a brilliant producer,

00:02:29.305 --> 00:02:31.507
a brilliant DJ, and he
puts on a weird mouse head,

00:02:31.507 --> 00:02:33.963
and everybody goes crazy.

00:02:33.963 --> 00:02:35.058
You know.

00:02:35.058 --> 00:02:38.450
So, that tells you the whole story.

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:40.831
And I think that's what you have to do.

00:02:40.831 --> 00:02:41.664
Look for--

00:02:42.535 --> 00:02:43.624
Look inside yourself.

00:02:43.624 --> 00:02:44.504
Who do I want to be?

00:02:44.504 --> 00:02:45.829
What do I want to represent?

00:02:45.829 --> 00:02:47.579
And what is my sound?

00:02:48.469 --> 00:02:50.020
And of course we all look at other DJs,

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:51.933
but if you want to add something,

00:02:51.933 --> 00:02:53.927
don't copy what's already out there,

00:02:53.927 --> 00:02:56.165
but try to make things--

00:02:56.165 --> 00:02:58.586
Make your sound current,
but make it your own.

00:02:58.586 --> 00:03:02.650
Sometimes an instrumental
tune can get in the charts,

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:03.599
but it won't get as high.

00:03:03.599 --> 00:03:06.672
Like Foo Focus was obviously
the first single from Mirage.

00:03:06.672 --> 00:03:09.039
I consciously made the choice
to make an instrumental track

00:03:09.039 --> 00:03:10.825
as the first single.

00:03:10.825 --> 00:03:12.057
That wasn't a commercial choice,

00:03:12.057 --> 00:03:13.713
I just wanted to show
the people hey, you know,

00:03:13.713 --> 00:03:15.602
this is what rocks the dance floor,

00:03:15.602 --> 00:03:17.031
and I know where I come from.

00:03:17.031 --> 00:03:19.261
But I think all music grows

00:03:19.261 --> 00:03:21.761
if you try to step over
your boundaries, you know.

00:03:21.761 --> 00:03:23.323
If you look at the history of music,

00:03:23.323 --> 00:03:24.806
whether it's Bach or Beethoven,

00:03:24.806 --> 00:03:27.107
or the Beatles and Bob Dylan.

00:03:27.107 --> 00:03:28.042
Or Pink Floyd.

00:03:28.042 --> 00:03:29.429
They all looked at each other,

00:03:29.429 --> 00:03:32.344
and music only became interesting

00:03:32.344 --> 00:03:34.204
when people stepped over boundaries.

00:03:34.204 --> 00:03:36.418
You see the same thing
happening now with David Guetta

00:03:36.418 --> 00:03:39.796
and all kinds of hip-hop
artists teaming up.

00:03:39.796 --> 00:03:41.896
I think it's only interesting for music.

00:03:41.896 --> 00:03:43.635
So I made the conscious choice

00:03:43.635 --> 00:03:46.798
to make half the album of Mirage
instrumental trance tunes;

00:03:46.798 --> 00:03:48.386
that's where I come
from, that's what I DJ,

00:03:48.386 --> 00:03:50.455
that's the sound that people know me for.

00:03:50.455 --> 00:03:51.296
That's what I like,

00:03:51.296 --> 00:03:53.555
tracks like Orbion, Coming Home, Mirage,

00:03:53.555 --> 00:03:54.590
and all that sort of stuff.

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:56.043
And then the other tracks
are just a little bit

00:03:56.043 --> 00:03:57.327
experimentation, you know.

00:03:57.327 --> 00:03:58.698
I like all kinds of music.

00:03:58.698 --> 00:04:00.738
I like the more housy stuff.

00:04:00.738 --> 00:04:02.928
I like the more techy stuff.

00:04:02.928 --> 00:04:04.242
I like a good song.

00:04:04.242 --> 00:04:07.521
So for me Mirage is
also a little bit of a--

00:04:07.521 --> 00:04:08.665
Ya, a little bit of a tryout

00:04:08.665 --> 00:04:11.446
to see if people like this
new direction that I'm taking.

00:04:11.446 --> 00:04:13.638
I think I'm the first DJ
that ever did a tryout show

00:04:13.638 --> 00:04:16.426
like what they do in theaters.

00:04:16.426 --> 00:04:18.173
But it was incredibly inspiring

00:04:18.173 --> 00:04:20.469
to work with such a big group of people,

00:04:20.469 --> 00:04:21.794
travel around the world, you know.

00:04:21.794 --> 00:04:23.573
Normally I would just show
up with a bag of records,

00:04:23.573 --> 00:04:26.161
and maybe my tour manager and a light guy.

00:04:26.161 --> 00:04:29.511
And now we travel the world
with a group of 35 people.

00:04:29.511 --> 00:04:30.769
And even though financially

00:04:30.769 --> 00:04:32.419
it was the worst year I've ever had,

00:04:32.419 --> 00:04:36.337
it was creatively, it was
such an inspiring thing.

00:04:36.337 --> 00:04:38.404
Because I learned so much.

00:04:38.404 --> 00:04:41.551
Actually my current DJ sets we
still use a time code system

00:04:41.551 --> 00:04:44.131
that we designed for the Armin Only show.

00:04:44.131 --> 00:04:45.118
So, you know,

00:04:45.118 --> 00:04:47.503
I learned so much that my
performances got better,

00:04:47.503 --> 00:04:48.357
thanks to that.

00:04:48.357 --> 00:04:49.404
And it's just,

00:04:49.404 --> 00:04:52.219
I think as an artist you
should show to the people

00:04:52.219 --> 00:04:55.278
that you don't want to be
stuck just doing one thing.

00:04:55.278 --> 00:04:57.073
As an artist you've got to develop,

00:04:57.073 --> 00:05:00.298
you have to get in touch with new sounds,

00:05:00.298 --> 00:05:01.210
try different things.

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:02.497
You know like on the Another You record

00:05:02.497 --> 00:05:04.829
with Mr. Probz for example.

00:05:04.829 --> 00:05:07.773
I hired an entire string orchestra.

00:05:07.773 --> 00:05:08.840
If you listen to the track,

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:11.093
the strings that are
in the track are real.

00:05:11.093 --> 00:05:12.258
So you can actually hear it,

00:05:12.258 --> 00:05:14.449
because they're not exactly on time.

00:05:14.449 --> 00:05:16.798
They're little bit off, but
that gives it the human feel.

00:05:16.798 --> 00:05:19.097
So I was actually conducting an orchestra.

00:05:19.097 --> 00:05:21.927
You know, din, din, din, din.

00:05:21.927 --> 00:05:25.498
And it's great because I'm
not a conductor, I'm not.

00:05:25.498 --> 00:05:27.200
I'm not a musician in that sense.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:28.359
But it's great to learn.

00:05:28.359 --> 00:05:30.139
I learned so much from that experience.

00:05:30.139 --> 00:05:32.531
And I think you should show
that to your fans, you know.

00:05:32.531 --> 00:05:34.237
I can't do the same thing twice,

00:05:34.237 --> 00:05:38.413
even though I have a lot of
nostalgic feelings for the past.

00:05:38.413 --> 00:05:40.479
I want to keep learning.

00:05:40.479 --> 00:05:42.715
And this is something that
I've invested a lot of time in.

00:05:42.715 --> 00:05:43.548
Musical--

00:05:43.548 --> 00:05:45.331
I've been educating myself a lot.

00:05:45.331 --> 00:05:47.517
On my iPad, following courses online,

00:05:47.517 --> 00:05:50.051
and learning a lot of new
techniques, new plug-ins,

00:05:50.051 --> 00:05:50.884
new sounds.

00:05:50.884 --> 00:05:53.995
So you have to do that to
stay fresh and stay focused.

00:05:53.995 --> 00:05:56.528
Recently I started touring
with this Armin Only concept,

00:05:56.528 --> 00:05:58.670
which we brought to Madison
Square Garden in New York,

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:01.606
and the Forum in L.A., and
a couple of other venues.

00:06:01.606 --> 00:06:03.868
It was really exciting to do
the idea behind the shows,

00:06:03.868 --> 00:06:06.185
that I'm the only DJ, hence Armin Only.

00:06:06.185 --> 00:06:07.409
But then we had the State of Trance,

00:06:07.409 --> 00:06:09.700
which is basically my own festival.

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:13.173
It's a radio show, that I do live events.

00:06:13.173 --> 00:06:14.665
The radio show is weekly,

00:06:14.665 --> 00:06:16.655
but then once a year I
throw a massive event

00:06:16.655 --> 00:06:17.961
with five areas.

00:06:17.961 --> 00:06:19.875
That's all broadcasted live and for free.

00:06:19.875 --> 00:06:21.542
So that's different.

00:06:22.556 --> 00:06:23.489
I'm just trying to evolve.

00:06:23.489 --> 00:06:25.403
You know, doing things that I like,

00:06:25.403 --> 00:06:28.708
things that make me
exciting, keep me excited,

00:06:28.708 --> 00:06:32.469
and you know, keep focused on the music.

00:06:32.469 --> 00:06:35.052
(upbeat music)

00:06:36.448 --> 00:06:37.988
It's all about, at the end of the day,

00:06:37.988 --> 00:06:39.917
what do you want to deliver as an artist?

00:06:39.917 --> 00:06:41.910
What do you want to bring,
what do you want to tell?

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:43.006
And it's so--

00:06:43.006 --> 00:06:46.629
I mean the thrill that you get of an idea.

00:06:46.629 --> 00:06:49.062
I mean I had the idea for
Ping Pong for example.

00:06:49.062 --> 00:06:51.869
(upbeat music)

00:06:51.869 --> 00:06:53.332
And I mean everything
in dance music right now

00:06:53.332 --> 00:06:54.340
is so serious.

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:56.844
(upbeat music)

00:06:56.844 --> 00:06:58.835
I wanted to add a little
bit of a fun element.

00:06:58.835 --> 00:07:01.418
(upbeat music)

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:06.884
I mean, it's a little
bit of an ironic track.

00:07:06.884 --> 00:07:09.467
(upbeat music)

00:07:14.481 --> 00:07:16.622
I did it in four hours.

00:07:16.622 --> 00:07:19.205
(upbeat music)

00:07:43.049 --> 00:07:44.822
Ping Pong was just, oh you know, silly.

00:07:44.822 --> 00:07:46.872
I didn't even want to
release it initially.

00:07:46.872 --> 00:07:49.796
But it's so great that with all my tracks,

00:07:49.796 --> 00:07:52.011
they're all my babies.

00:07:52.011 --> 00:07:54.309
And you see a crowd going crazy to an idea

00:07:54.309 --> 00:07:57.559
that you had in your head, and they go.

00:07:58.506 --> 00:08:00.668
That gives the biggest kick.

00:08:00.668 --> 00:08:02.501
Self-education is key.

00:08:03.853 --> 00:08:05.896
Why not, instead of
watching another series

00:08:05.896 --> 00:08:07.482
that everybody is talking about,

00:08:07.482 --> 00:08:09.818
use that exact time and go online

00:08:09.818 --> 00:08:11.649
and watch a couple of these tutorials?

00:08:11.649 --> 00:08:13.703
Because that's what inspires me.

00:08:13.703 --> 00:08:16.172
I learned a lot of tricks from producing,

00:08:16.172 --> 00:08:17.904
making specific sounds.

00:08:17.904 --> 00:08:19.136
You know, how can I make this pad,

00:08:19.136 --> 00:08:20.427
and make it sound really wide?

00:08:20.427 --> 00:08:22.445
How can I make this trance riff?

00:08:22.445 --> 00:08:23.726
How do you program that?

00:08:23.726 --> 00:08:25.967
How can I make my kick sound really phat?

00:08:25.967 --> 00:08:29.186
How can I have that clap with
the big reverb behind it,

00:08:29.186 --> 00:08:30.429
and reverse it?

00:08:30.429 --> 00:08:31.567
And how do you do that?

00:08:31.567 --> 00:08:33.319
How do I arrange my song?

00:08:33.319 --> 00:08:35.172
Do I have enough elements
to arrange my song?

00:08:35.172 --> 00:08:38.155
All these questions, and a million more.

00:08:38.155 --> 00:08:40.902
You find answers online.

00:08:40.902 --> 00:08:41.971
I promise you,

00:08:41.971 --> 00:08:44.002
as soon as you finish a
couple of these courses,

00:08:44.002 --> 00:08:45.277
you'll be so inspired,

00:08:45.277 --> 00:08:47.610
and the music will start writing itself.

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:50.042
(upbeat music)

00:08:50.042 --> 00:08:52.283
Well before we start doing this,

00:08:52.283 --> 00:08:55.337
we had a massive rehearsal time,

00:08:55.337 --> 00:08:58.719
and we hired the (mumbles)
in the Netherlands.

00:08:58.719 --> 00:09:00.768
We flew all the artists
to the Netherlands.

00:09:00.768 --> 00:09:02.641
And for three weeks we rehearsed.

00:09:02.641 --> 00:09:04.861
So every single track was
rehearsed with staging.

00:09:04.861 --> 00:09:07.292
We had the entire stage
there, and no crowd.

00:09:07.292 --> 00:09:08.688
And then we did tryout shows.

00:09:08.688 --> 00:09:11.506
We had two tryouts in
front of a live audience.

00:09:11.506 --> 00:09:14.147
And then you immediately
see where the problems are.

00:09:14.147 --> 00:09:16.489
So then we again, we rehearsed
and rehearsed, and rehearsed.

00:09:16.489 --> 00:09:18.334
To get it better and better, and better.

00:09:18.334 --> 00:09:20.724
And then we did the premier show

00:09:20.724 --> 00:09:21.961
at the Ziggo Dome in Amsterdam.

00:09:21.961 --> 00:09:24.227
We did the show for the very first time.

00:09:24.227 --> 00:09:27.022
And you see that the show gets
better every time we do it.

00:09:27.022 --> 00:09:29.125
Also my freestyles change every time.

00:09:29.125 --> 00:09:30.670
So it's exciting for the crew,

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:32.253
because they don't hear
the same music every show.

00:09:32.253 --> 00:09:34.597
So every Armin Only show
that I do is different.

00:09:34.597 --> 00:09:37.461
The track list from the
show I did in Oakland,

00:09:37.461 --> 00:09:38.367
was different than the track list

00:09:38.367 --> 00:09:39.753
from the show I did in L.A.

00:09:39.753 --> 00:09:42.941
So it makes it exciting for everybody.

00:09:42.941 --> 00:09:45.713
And the crew feels really relaxed,

00:09:45.713 --> 00:09:48.994
because they know exactly
what's going to happen.

00:09:48.994 --> 00:09:52.350
So if I select a track
with a live element,

00:09:52.350 --> 00:09:55.023
my stage manager backstage
sees it on his iPad,

00:09:55.023 --> 00:09:56.945
he sees what tracks I select.

00:09:56.945 --> 00:10:00.671
And then the next artist will be ready.

00:10:00.671 --> 00:10:02.787
And the artist knows,

00:10:02.787 --> 00:10:05.280
okay whatever track he has running before,

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:08.659
he knows that he or she
needs to walk on stage

00:10:08.659 --> 00:10:10.076
at one minute 40.

00:10:11.086 --> 00:10:12.276
So everybody's prepared.

00:10:12.276 --> 00:10:13.512
And preparation is key.

00:10:13.512 --> 00:10:14.401
And that's what I learned

00:10:14.401 --> 00:10:16.339
from working with a theater director.

00:10:16.339 --> 00:10:20.012
You know, so instead
of the awkward feeling

00:10:20.012 --> 00:10:21.978
like sort of pushing a
microphone in a mixer

00:10:21.978 --> 00:10:23.678
and say hey, okay sing.

00:10:23.678 --> 00:10:24.511
Go, sing.

00:10:25.348 --> 00:10:27.181
Instead of doing that,

00:10:28.559 --> 00:10:31.240
we make sure that the
artist gets vocal coaching,

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:33.700
an artist is relaxed, an
artist gets treated well.

00:10:33.700 --> 00:10:34.900
Gets a good hotel room.

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:36.881
Pick-ups, everything is
organized and arranged.

00:10:36.881 --> 00:10:38.691
But it's a big operation.

00:10:38.691 --> 00:10:40.540
I don't feel God is a DJ.

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:41.621
I don't like that expression.

00:10:41.621 --> 00:10:44.352
I feel I'm really part
of the crowd in a way.

00:10:44.352 --> 00:10:46.566
I try to enjoy the music
as much as possible.

00:10:46.566 --> 00:10:49.096
Because I notice that
if I don't enjoy myself,

00:10:49.096 --> 00:10:51.621
the crowd is more flat as well, you know.

00:10:51.621 --> 00:10:53.465
People expect me to enjoy myself.

00:10:53.465 --> 00:10:56.062
And I'm enjoying the music just as much,

00:10:56.062 --> 00:10:57.359
or even more than the crowd,

00:10:57.359 --> 00:10:58.867
because I'm really, really into it.

00:10:58.867 --> 00:11:00.782
I feel that's my assignment.

00:11:00.782 --> 00:11:02.670
And I want to stress that the word passion

00:11:02.670 --> 00:11:03.996
is probably the most important

00:11:03.996 --> 00:11:07.145
in that you have to really, really,

00:11:07.145 --> 00:11:08.839
really want something.

00:11:08.839 --> 00:11:10.457
And I think in life,

00:11:10.457 --> 00:11:13.207
it's so much more easy if you are devoted

00:11:13.207 --> 00:11:14.693
and dedicated to something.

00:11:14.693 --> 00:11:18.283
If you like your job, no not
like your job, love your job,

00:11:18.283 --> 00:11:20.467
you'll be 200% good at it.

00:11:20.467 --> 00:11:22.251
Because you're passionate about it.

00:11:22.251 --> 00:11:24.492
So ask yourself on a daily basis,

00:11:24.492 --> 00:11:28.254
is what I'm doing right
now, is it something I love.

00:11:28.254 --> 00:11:30.827
Because if you don't
love what you're doing,

00:11:30.827 --> 00:11:32.821
you're actually wasting your time.

00:11:32.821 --> 00:11:34.568
You should stop doing what you do.

00:11:34.568 --> 00:11:36.738
You should try and search
for something else.

00:11:36.738 --> 00:11:39.833
Because I'm convinced that if
you don't love what you do,

00:11:39.833 --> 00:11:41.933
you're only doing at

00:11:41.933 --> 00:11:42.959
maybe at 50

00:11:42.959 --> 00:11:45.709
or 70% of what you could do.

00:11:46.651 --> 00:11:48.163
And if you love what you do,

00:11:48.163 --> 00:11:49.834
you're really passionate
about what you do,

00:11:49.834 --> 00:11:51.347
you love you job or you love your work,

00:11:51.347 --> 00:11:53.111
or you love whatever you're doing,

00:11:53.111 --> 00:11:55.024
then you'll go for it 200%.

00:11:55.024 --> 00:11:56.858
And then you'll see that
you can really excel.

00:11:56.858 --> 00:12:01.255
You can reach stuff beyond
what you expected you would.

00:12:01.255 --> 00:12:03.019
I mean, people always ask me like,

00:12:03.019 --> 00:12:05.111
why do you do eight hour DJ sets?

00:12:05.111 --> 00:12:06.858
It's because I love it man.

00:12:06.858 --> 00:12:08.145
I want to play all this music.

00:12:08.145 --> 00:12:10.313
And then, it physically,

00:12:10.313 --> 00:12:12.670
it's not healthy to not go to the bathroom

00:12:12.670 --> 00:12:14.381
for six, seven hours.

00:12:14.381 --> 00:12:16.732
And I do it because

00:12:16.732 --> 00:12:19.134
I just don't think about
going to the bathroom.

00:12:19.134 --> 00:12:21.710
I just feel the need to perform.

00:12:21.710 --> 00:12:22.685
Like, I'm in the zone.

00:12:22.685 --> 00:12:23.518
I'm in the moment.

00:12:23.518 --> 00:12:24.906
I want to rock that crowd.

00:12:24.906 --> 00:12:26.977
And that's what I mean, you excel

00:12:26.977 --> 00:12:28.203
You go beyond your--

00:12:28.203 --> 00:12:31.516
A human being can really achieve--

00:12:31.516 --> 00:12:33.258
It's like what Obi-Wan Kenobi said.

00:12:33.258 --> 00:12:35.994
You know, "If you can put your mind to it,

00:12:35.994 --> 00:12:37.967
you can accomplish anything."

00:12:37.967 --> 00:12:40.235
But you have to understand for
me, when I'm in the studio,

00:12:40.235 --> 00:12:42.312
I'm not thinking about
creating a hit record,

00:12:42.312 --> 00:12:44.773
or creating a pop record
like you were saying.

00:12:44.773 --> 00:12:45.606
- You're creating what you want to create.

00:12:45.606 --> 00:12:47.698
- I'm just doing something
that comes from my heart.

00:12:47.698 --> 00:12:48.531
- Okay.

00:12:48.531 --> 00:12:49.485
- And sometimes it will please you,

00:12:49.485 --> 00:12:50.785
and sometimes it won't.

00:12:50.785 --> 00:12:51.618
- That's okay.

00:12:51.618 --> 00:12:55.153
- So one day I'll produce
a track for the Gaya guys,

00:12:55.153 --> 00:12:58.944
one day I'll make 1-3-8,
a Game of Thrones re-mix.

00:12:58.944 --> 00:13:03.111
And then the next day I'll
be making a down tempo track.

00:13:03.978 --> 00:13:05.701
It's good to have a plan.

00:13:05.701 --> 00:13:09.947
It's good to have a clear
idea of where you want to go.

00:13:09.947 --> 00:13:11.646
But along that--

00:13:11.646 --> 00:13:14.790
If you take that path, and
along that road can be--

00:13:14.790 --> 00:13:15.970
It can be bumpy.

00:13:15.970 --> 00:13:17.718
And sometimes the road's blocked,

00:13:17.718 --> 00:13:18.959
and you have to go somewhere else,

00:13:18.959 --> 00:13:21.126
you have to take a detour.

00:13:23.353 --> 00:13:27.870
I think it's really important
to look after your health.

00:13:27.870 --> 00:13:30.159
Really, don't smoke.

00:13:30.159 --> 00:13:32.197
Don't drink too much.

00:13:32.197 --> 00:13:33.608
Take care of you own body, you know.

00:13:33.608 --> 00:13:36.258
Your body is your temple.

00:13:36.258 --> 00:13:41.008
It's the only thing that's
with you all the time.

00:13:41.008 --> 00:13:42.658
So the past is who you are,

00:13:42.658 --> 00:13:45.241
but you cannot change the past.

00:13:48.004 --> 00:13:50.831
You know, in my job I'm always
looking for the next gig,

00:13:50.831 --> 00:13:52.796
and the next album, and the next tour.

00:13:52.796 --> 00:13:56.725
So I'm always living like
two, three months ahead.

00:13:56.725 --> 00:13:58.250
But I want to be more in the now.

00:13:58.250 --> 00:14:00.100
You know, when I'm with
my family on a holiday,

00:14:00.100 --> 00:14:00.947
when we're having dinner,

00:14:00.947 --> 00:14:02.398
when we're going to
the beach or something.

00:14:02.398 --> 00:14:04.974
I wish I could just stop this head,

00:14:04.974 --> 00:14:06.074
you know, stop the train,

00:14:06.074 --> 00:14:07.574
and be in the now.

00:14:10.098 --> 00:14:14.590
For me, I can say that trance
is emotional, uplifting,

00:14:14.590 --> 00:14:15.906
mostly instrumental music

00:14:15.906 --> 00:14:18.029
that just touches your soul a little bit.

00:14:18.029 --> 00:14:20.853
It's very close to
classical music, you know.

00:14:20.853 --> 00:14:23.515
It's about the melody more than the beat.

00:14:23.515 --> 00:14:25.621
It's about the, yeah,

00:14:25.621 --> 00:14:29.204
the flow that you get
into listening to it.

00:14:30.818 --> 00:14:31.651
You just know.

00:14:31.651 --> 00:14:32.581
You know when you're in love.

00:14:32.581 --> 00:14:33.720
It's just very simple.

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:38.096
It's one of those strange
things in life, you know.

00:14:38.096 --> 00:14:40.276
I mean, anybody that's
married can tell you this.

00:14:40.276 --> 00:14:41.228
It's so easy to fall into the trap

00:14:41.228 --> 00:14:44.017
of being roommates with
each other, you know.

00:14:44.017 --> 00:14:46.610
So me and my wife, we go on dates.

00:14:46.610 --> 00:14:48.384
We totally brought back date night.

00:14:48.384 --> 00:14:50.634
(laughing)

00:14:52.595 --> 00:14:53.588
Doing a big show,

00:14:53.588 --> 00:14:55.886
especially when I did the Armin Only shows

00:14:55.886 --> 00:14:57.228
from my last album Intense,

00:14:57.228 --> 00:14:58.259
and now for the upcoming album

00:14:58.259 --> 00:15:02.578
when I want to do a new
tour called Embrace.

00:15:02.578 --> 00:15:04.419
It's so much energy that you see

00:15:04.419 --> 00:15:06.670
when you see these people in front of you.

00:15:06.670 --> 00:15:08.401
You get so much love from that crowd.

00:15:08.401 --> 00:15:10.176
But you always have to
pay a price somewhere.

00:15:10.176 --> 00:15:12.853
So the day after for
such a big performance,

00:15:12.853 --> 00:15:14.020
I'm empty man.

00:15:17.425 --> 00:15:19.086
When I get up in the
morning, the first thing,

00:15:19.086 --> 00:15:20.403
that knock that goes through my head,

00:15:20.403 --> 00:15:22.518
is not like oh how can
I make a million bucks.

00:15:22.518 --> 00:15:24.907
Or how can I be number
one DJ in the world.

00:15:24.907 --> 00:15:26.255
That's now what goes through my mind.

00:15:26.255 --> 00:15:29.224
I get up thinking hey,
Mr. Probz is coming over,

00:15:29.224 --> 00:15:30.261
and we're doing a track together.

00:15:30.261 --> 00:15:31.654
How exciting.

00:15:31.654 --> 00:15:33.512
And that's really what moves me.

00:15:33.512 --> 00:15:35.316
The motivation is to
have fun in the studio.

00:15:35.316 --> 00:15:37.073
And I understand that now.

00:15:37.073 --> 00:15:40.624
It's so important to go into
the studio and have fun.

00:15:40.624 --> 00:15:41.952
- I find it hard to believe

00:15:41.952 --> 00:15:44.342
that you have the time to you know,

00:15:44.342 --> 00:15:47.827
start working on an album,
start doing collaborations,

00:15:47.827 --> 00:15:49.131
but also at the same time

00:15:49.131 --> 00:15:51.489
how do you pick your collaborators?

00:15:51.489 --> 00:15:52.572
It's sort of;

00:15:54.430 --> 00:15:55.775
it just happens really.

00:15:55.775 --> 00:15:57.824
You meet somebody, you say
hey let's do a track together.

00:15:57.824 --> 00:16:01.117
You send each other a
text message, or an email.

00:16:01.117 --> 00:16:02.185
And you're trying to find a date,

00:16:02.185 --> 00:16:03.206
you sit in the studio together.

00:16:03.206 --> 00:16:04.339
I mean, honestly,

00:16:04.339 --> 00:16:06.169
half of the collaborations
that I tried to set up

00:16:06.169 --> 00:16:07.002
didn't work out.

00:16:07.002 --> 00:16:08.145
Simply because the artist didn't want to,

00:16:08.145 --> 00:16:10.964
there was no time, or
had other priorities.

00:16:10.964 --> 00:16:12.499
One of the collaborations I'm really proud

00:16:12.499 --> 00:16:14.659
is actually not on Embrace.

00:16:14.659 --> 00:16:16.820
It's with Jean-Michel Jarre,
the synthesizer legend.

00:16:16.820 --> 00:16:18.352
For his album, he approached me,

00:16:18.352 --> 00:16:21.937
and he said I want to do a track
with you for my next album.

00:16:21.937 --> 00:16:24.520
(upbeat music)

00:16:29.713 --> 00:16:31.682
I remember I was a little kid

00:16:31.682 --> 00:16:33.217
going through my dad's record collection,

00:16:33.217 --> 00:16:37.822
and seeing these humongous
covers of Jean-Michel Jarre.

00:16:37.822 --> 00:16:39.895
So, you know the day
that I could tell my dad

00:16:39.895 --> 00:16:42.347
that I was actually working
with the legend himself,

00:16:42.347 --> 00:16:43.860
was unbelievable

00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:45.985
So we created a track together
that I'm really proud of,

00:16:45.985 --> 00:16:46.818
called Stardust.

00:16:46.818 --> 00:16:49.845
Has more of my older trancing
melodies, my older sound.

00:16:49.845 --> 00:16:51.750
And it was so much fun to do as well.

00:16:51.750 --> 00:16:53.725
So this is what I want
to show you, you know.

00:16:53.725 --> 00:16:57.131
In one year, working
with Jean-Michel Jarre,

00:16:57.131 --> 00:16:58.530
working with Hardwell,

00:16:58.530 --> 00:17:00.185
working with Mr. Probz,

00:17:00.185 --> 00:17:01.932
working with a jazz trumpet.

00:17:01.932 --> 00:17:03.458
I mean, that's fun.

00:17:03.458 --> 00:17:06.754
I mean, you go into the
studio, and you're like wow.

00:17:06.754 --> 00:17:10.066
It's not just another
singer, or another you know,

00:17:10.066 --> 00:17:12.452
another celebrity who
wants to work with you.

00:17:12.452 --> 00:17:14.823
No, it's about you go into the studio,

00:17:14.823 --> 00:17:16.939
you try and do different things.

00:17:16.939 --> 00:17:18.221
And you try different sounds,

00:17:18.221 --> 00:17:20.205
and you go out of your
comfort zone a little bit,

00:17:20.205 --> 00:17:21.251
and that challenges me.

00:17:21.251 --> 00:17:22.549
I mean, I'm so inspired right now.

00:17:22.549 --> 00:17:25.021
I could easily, if you put
me in a studio right now,

00:17:25.021 --> 00:17:26.938
in a year from now I'll
have another album.

00:17:26.938 --> 00:17:28.082
I have so many ideas,

00:17:28.082 --> 00:17:29.640
and that's because I'm having so much fun

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:31.327
in the studio right now.

00:17:31.327 --> 00:17:33.803
Last time I was here, I
told Ferry I had an idea

00:17:33.803 --> 00:17:35.081
to use this drum kit,

00:17:35.081 --> 00:17:38.498
because it sounds like a march, you know.

00:17:41.326 --> 00:17:43.620
Like, you hear the army coming.

00:17:43.620 --> 00:17:44.944
The big break is coming.

00:17:44.944 --> 00:17:46.730
The war is about to start.

00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:49.900
So you hear the army further away.

00:17:49.900 --> 00:17:52.567
(intense music)

00:18:02.255 --> 00:18:04.906
- This track is going
to be on the next album.

00:18:04.906 --> 00:18:06.373
But like Armin said,

00:18:06.373 --> 00:18:10.022
this is an addition to the actual track.

00:18:10.022 --> 00:18:12.027
It's just a little break,
like a little teaser.

00:18:12.027 --> 00:18:13.522
So it's not that you're
listening to a song

00:18:13.522 --> 00:18:15.684
that's only doing this in the album.

00:18:15.684 --> 00:18:18.178
(intense music)

00:18:18.178 --> 00:18:20.210
- Loud. (laughing)

00:18:20.210 --> 00:18:22.877
(intense music)

00:18:27.089 --> 00:18:28.561
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:18:28.561 --> 00:18:31.709
I made this video because
DJ Valence asked me to.

00:18:31.709 --> 00:18:32.798
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:18:32.798 --> 00:18:34.223
that you want me to profile next,

00:18:34.223 --> 00:18:35.668
check out the link in the description,

00:18:35.668 --> 00:18:37.808
and you can go and cast your vote.

00:18:37.808 --> 00:18:38.641
I'd also love to know

00:18:38.641 --> 00:18:40.430
which clip resonated the most with you.

00:18:40.430 --> 00:18:42.345
What lesson are you going
to take from this video

00:18:42.345 --> 00:18:44.401
and immediately apply to your life

00:18:44.401 --> 00:18:45.765
or to your business somehow?

00:18:45.765 --> 00:18:47.135
Leave it down in the comments below.

00:18:47.135 --> 00:18:49.530
I'm really curious to find
out what you have to say.

00:18:49.530 --> 00:18:52.020
I also want to give a quick
shout-out to Lucas Alexander.

00:18:52.020 --> 00:18:54.528
Lucas, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:18:54.528 --> 00:18:57.797
Your One Word, and posting
that picture to Instagram.

00:18:57.797 --> 00:18:59.578
I really appreciate the support man.

00:18:59.578 --> 00:19:01.594
And I'm so glad that you enjoyed my book.

00:19:01.594 --> 00:19:02.897
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:19:02.897 --> 00:19:04.074
I believe in you.

00:19:04.074 --> 00:19:06.054
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:19:06.054 --> 00:19:08.173
and whatever your one word is.

00:19:08.173 --> 00:19:09.683
Much love.

00:19:09.683 --> 00:19:11.192
I'll see you soon.

00:19:11.192 --> 00:19:13.609
(jet engine)

00:19:16.914 --> 00:19:20.392
- We had 10,000 plus people
in, so you saw it yourself.

00:19:20.392 --> 00:19:21.225
- Yes.
- It was crazy.

00:19:21.225 --> 00:19:23.015
- And then also we had a vinyl set.

00:19:23.015 --> 00:19:24.558
- A vinyl set, yeah.

00:19:24.558 --> 00:19:26.992
- What's the idea behind the vinyl set?

00:19:26.992 --> 00:19:28.210
- Well, I always see Armin Only

00:19:28.210 --> 00:19:32.135
as Christmas dinner with
Armin van Buuren, so you know.

00:19:32.135 --> 00:19:33.992
I also want to show my fans

00:19:33.992 --> 00:19:35.596
that I'll never forget where I come from.

00:19:35.596 --> 00:19:36.429
- Right.

00:19:36.429 --> 00:19:38.814
- And instead of playing
re-works of the classics,

00:19:38.814 --> 00:19:40.011
I was just like you know,

00:19:40.011 --> 00:19:41.587
why not just play the original vinyl.

00:19:41.587 --> 00:19:45.346
Which brings back the original
sound of the classics.

00:19:45.346 --> 00:19:48.307
And I always felt that vinyl
added something special.

00:19:48.307 --> 00:19:50.161
The sound of vinyl added something special

00:19:50.161 --> 00:19:51.287
to the trance music.

00:19:51.287 --> 00:19:52.661
Because actually,

00:19:52.661 --> 00:19:55.435
you're getting less
information than from the CD,

00:19:55.435 --> 00:19:56.768
or from USB key.

00:19:58.437 --> 00:20:00.491
Because the sound characteristics of vinyl

00:20:00.491 --> 00:20:05.141
they cut away the parts
of the frequency spectrum.

00:20:05.141 --> 00:20:07.225
But it just adds something
to the sound, you know.

00:20:07.225 --> 00:20:10.966
And yeah, it's a little
gift to my fans I guess.

00:20:10.966 --> 00:20:13.549
(upbeat music)

00:20:15.756 --> 00:20:16.872
Love it.

00:20:16.872 --> 00:20:18.487
I mean, you know,

00:20:18.487 --> 00:20:20.340
I've been DJ'ing for such
a long time in my career,

00:20:20.340 --> 00:20:22.174
that you know, to keep it interesting

00:20:22.174 --> 00:20:23.353
and exciting for myself,

00:20:23.353 --> 00:20:25.809
I love doing various things.

00:20:25.809 --> 00:20:28.825
So, what keeps me excited
for all these years,

00:20:28.825 --> 00:20:30.136
is of course the music, the fans.

00:20:30.136 --> 00:20:33.135
But also, you know doing
all these different shows,

00:20:33.135 --> 00:20:34.759
and trying different things.

00:20:34.759 --> 00:20:37.131
And I mean, a show like Armin Only

00:20:37.131 --> 00:20:41.865
is not just the way to
promote the music or whatever,

00:20:41.865 --> 00:20:45.791
but it's really also for
myself to keep it exciting.

00:20:45.791 --> 00:20:46.874
And you know,

00:20:48.483 --> 00:20:49.509
I want to wake up in the morning

00:20:49.509 --> 00:20:52.059
thinking that wow, I'm going
to go to L.A. and do the show.

00:20:52.059 --> 00:20:54.013
I'm going to bring this
person, and that person.

00:20:54.013 --> 00:20:55.846
The secret to success.

00:20:56.788 --> 00:20:59.744
There's a couple of elements
that are important in success.

00:20:59.744 --> 00:21:03.244
First of all is your own
level of expectation.

00:21:03.244 --> 00:21:05.006
You also have to learn to be happy

00:21:05.006 --> 00:21:07.319
with what you achieve sometimes.

00:21:07.319 --> 00:21:09.418
It's impossible in life to always achieve

00:21:09.418 --> 00:21:11.343
exactly what you have in
the back of your head.

00:21:11.343 --> 00:21:13.668
So you need to learn also to be happy

00:21:13.668 --> 00:21:14.910
with what you achieved.

00:21:14.910 --> 00:21:19.077
Even if it's only half of what
you had in mind, at first.

00:21:20.563 --> 00:21:22.371
I like a quote of John Lennon,

00:21:22.371 --> 00:21:24.615
"Life's what's happening to you

00:21:24.615 --> 00:21:26.513
when you're busy making other plans."

00:21:26.513 --> 00:21:28.835
And that is what I keep
telling my wife as well.

00:21:28.835 --> 00:21:30.328
And a whole lot of people around me.

00:21:30.328 --> 00:21:31.728
You always expect something,

00:21:31.728 --> 00:21:33.054
and then something along the way comes,

00:21:33.054 --> 00:21:35.288
and then you have to
alter your path again.

00:21:35.288 --> 00:21:37.247
And if you can learn to live with that,

00:21:37.247 --> 00:21:38.961
if you can be practical like that,

00:21:38.961 --> 00:21:40.843
then success is more easy, maybe.

00:21:40.843 --> 00:21:42.292
It's good to have a plan,

00:21:42.292 --> 00:21:44.693
but it's also good if
you're working really hard

00:21:44.693 --> 00:21:46.457
and you're walking up against a door

00:21:46.457 --> 00:21:48.236
and you can't go through that door,

00:21:48.236 --> 00:21:50.865
then you just find a backdoor.

00:21:50.865 --> 00:21:52.587
It's find your way around it.

00:21:52.587 --> 00:21:54.043
And that's just the way that life is.

00:21:54.043 --> 00:21:57.247
Sometimes you cannot get
exactly what you want,

00:21:57.247 --> 00:21:59.375
or what you had in the back of your mind.

00:21:59.375 --> 00:22:00.569
Yes, it's very important

00:22:00.569 --> 00:22:02.420
that you have to have a good work ethic.

00:22:02.420 --> 00:22:04.394
Nobody gets anywhere without hard work.

00:22:04.394 --> 00:22:06.457
And you'll have good and bad days.

00:22:06.457 --> 00:22:08.664
Even myself as a producer,

00:22:08.664 --> 00:22:11.913
I run into disappointments
almost on a daily basis.

00:22:11.913 --> 00:22:13.299
Because I try to work with artists

00:22:13.299 --> 00:22:16.120
that on the last moment
pull back their song.

00:22:16.120 --> 00:22:19.051
You don't see that; you
don't read that in the news.

00:22:19.051 --> 00:22:20.340
But it's a fact of the matter.

00:22:20.340 --> 00:22:22.484
And I think all artists have
to deal with stuff like that.

00:22:22.484 --> 00:22:24.169
You don't get the set times you want.

00:22:24.169 --> 00:22:25.516
You don't get the billing you want.

00:22:25.516 --> 00:22:27.843
You don't get the hotel
room that you requested.

00:22:27.843 --> 00:22:29.255
It's something that you have to deal with.

00:22:29.255 --> 00:22:31.075
And it, you know, defines your character,

00:22:31.075 --> 00:22:33.410
how easy you are to deal
with stuff like that.

00:22:33.410 --> 00:22:36.579
You know, life is a roller
coaster ride for everybody.

00:22:36.579 --> 00:22:38.277
It has its ups and its downs.

00:22:38.277 --> 00:22:39.669
But if everything goes well,

00:22:39.669 --> 00:22:41.209
you'll have more ups than downs,

00:22:41.209 --> 00:22:42.178
and you'll remember the ups

00:22:42.178 --> 00:22:44.181
more than you'll remember the downs.

00:22:44.181 --> 00:22:45.757
But it's a fact of the
matter that everybody,

00:22:45.757 --> 00:22:48.094
no matter if you're a big
artist, a successful artist,

00:22:48.094 --> 00:22:49.483
or a successful businessman,

00:22:49.483 --> 00:22:51.689
everybody works hard to get somewhere.

00:22:51.689 --> 00:22:52.522
It's not about, you know,

00:22:52.522 --> 00:22:54.405
how many Grammy nominations you have,

00:22:54.405 --> 00:22:57.147
or how many time you
won DJ Mag, or whatever.

00:22:57.147 --> 00:22:58.144
It's not about that.

00:22:58.144 --> 00:23:00.722
At that point you're just
trying to rock the crowd.

00:23:00.722 --> 00:23:02.765
You're trying to interact with people,

00:23:02.765 --> 00:23:03.875
and it's so beautiful.

00:23:03.875 --> 00:23:06.029
I mean, to feel that
connection with a crowd,

00:23:06.029 --> 00:23:08.376
seeing all those people having
the best time of their lives.

00:23:08.376 --> 00:23:11.031
They're in an office during
the week, and they dress up,

00:23:11.031 --> 00:23:13.155
and they go out,

00:23:13.155 --> 00:23:17.322
and you get to be the guy
that gets to entertain them.

00:23:18.348 --> 00:23:19.525
That is so beautiful.

00:23:19.525 --> 00:23:22.283
I mean, from all the things
that I'm doing in my career.

00:23:22.283 --> 00:23:25.116
Jet lag, airports, waiting, taxes,

00:23:26.628 --> 00:23:27.518
everything.

00:23:27.518 --> 00:23:29.074
From all the things
that I do in my career,

00:23:29.074 --> 00:23:33.169
that is the only thing that
I'm still not bored with.

00:23:33.169 --> 00:23:34.365
It's a thrill.

00:23:34.365 --> 00:23:36.112
Why are the Rolling Stones still playing

00:23:36.112 --> 00:23:37.679
when they're 70 years old?

00:23:37.679 --> 00:23:39.990
It's because you can never
get enough of that kick,

00:23:39.990 --> 00:23:41.580
of the love of the crowd.

00:23:41.580 --> 00:23:43.774
It's an addiction, for sure.

